The best component does not automatically become the control point

A control point is a layer that other participants have difficulty changing without disrupting the useful system. Scarcity can create temporary bargaining power, but durable control usually requires more: the component must sit at an important boundary, replacing it must force costly work elsewhere, and one platform must learn or qualify that boundary faster than alternatives.

That is why a whole AI rack is a better test than a parts list. An accelerator, memory stack, scale-up fabric, network card, power shelf, cooling manifold and software stack can each meet its own specification while the assembled system still misses performance, reliability or deployment targets. The control point forms where someone turns those separate claims into repeatable useful output.

An integrated stack compresses the qualification path

NVIDIA's documented Vera Rubin NVL72 rack combines 72 Rubin GPUs with Vera CPUs, nine NVLink 6 switch trays, ConnectX-9 interfaces, BlueField-4 data-processing units, local storage, power shelves, busbars and liquid-cooling manifolds. The backplane, tray design, switch topology, management controllers and cooling path are engineered as one system.

That integration can shorten debugging. Operators can trace software, GPU scheduling, link traffic, thermals and system telemetry through one platform, while suppliers accumulate evidence across repeated builds.

NVIDIA said on May 31, 2026 that Vera Rubin was ramping into full production across major system builders and that Spectrum-X Ethernet Photonics was in production. That is stronger evidence than a roadmap, although NVIDIA's performance comparisons remain supplier claims without comparable customer results.

An open challenger moves the boundary rather than eliminating it

AMD Helios illustrates a different approach. Its current reference design combines 72 MI455X accelerators, EPYC CPUs and Pensando networking while using UALink over Ethernet for scale-up, Ultra Ethernet-aligned networking for scale-out and the Open Compute Project's Open Rack Wide form factor. The aim is to let multiple manufacturers and component suppliers build around shared interfaces instead of one proprietary rack boundary.

Open specifications can reduce dependence on a single interface owner. UALink publishes rules for accelerator links, physical layers and management, so a customer can in principle change one layer while preserving the surrounding contract.

But a specification is not a qualified system. AMD describes Helios as a reference design rather than a product sold directly, with partner-built volume systems expected in the second half of 2026. Firmware, switching, cooling, security, software and service procedures still must work together under real workloads. If one integrator becomes the only party able to make those pieces dependable, the control point has moved from the proprietary interface to system qualification and support.

Three tests reveal where control actually sits

